  "content": {
    "id": "v2-25d81-p66-practice-slot-14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "For each curve, find the $x$-intercepts and the gradient of the curve at each intercept. State whether the curve is rising or falling there.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-25d81-p66-practice-slot-14-slot-14-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=\\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Intercepts $x=3$ and $x=-2$. At $x=3$ the gradient is $\\dfrac57$ (rising); at $x=-2$ the gradient is $-\\dfrac52$ (falling).",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}0&=x^2-x-6\\\\0&=(x-3)(x+2)\\\\x&=3,-2\\end{align*}$$\n$u=x^2-x-6$, $u'=2x-1$, $v=x+4$, $v'=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{(2x-1)(x+4)-(x^2-x-6)(1)}{(x+4)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{x^2+8x+2}{(x+4)^2}\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}y'(3)&=\\frac{35}{49}=\\frac57>0\\\\y'(-2)&=\\frac{-10}{4}=-\\frac52<0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is rising at $x=3$ and falling at $x=-2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-25d81-p66-practice-slot-14-slot-14-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=\\dfrac{4-x^2}{x^2+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Intercepts $x=2$ and $x=-2$. At $x=2$ the gradient is $-\\dfrac12$ (falling); at $x=-2$ the gradient is $\\dfrac12$ (rising).",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}0&=4-x^2\\\\x&=\\pm2\\end{align*}$$\n$u=4-x^2$, $u'=-2x$, $v=x^2+4$, $v'=2x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{-2x(x^2+4)-(4-x^2)(2x)}{(x^2+4)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{-2x^3-8x-8x+2x^3}{(x^2+4)^2}\\\\y'&=-\\frac{16x}{(x^2+4)^2}\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}y'(2)&=-\\frac{32}{64}=-\\frac12<0\\\\y'(-2)&=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac12>0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is falling at $x=2$ and rising at $x=-2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      }
    ]
  },
